      "math_academy_id": "q-81229",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}+8{b}_{2} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})+8(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
